  "final_response": "- To create a new branch you run  \n\n  ```bash\n  git branch <new-branch-name>\n  ```  \n\n  This command creates a new pointer that starts at the commit you’re currently on but **does not** change the branch you’re on; you remain on your current branch (e.g., `master`). [nutshell.002-creating-a-new-branch]\n\n- If you want to create a branch **and** switch to it in a single step, you can use  \n\n  ```bash\n  git checkout -b <new-branch-name>\n  ```  \n\n  This creates the new branch and checks it out immediately. [nutshell.003-switching-branches]\n\n  (From Git 2.23 onward the equivalent modern command is `git switch -c <new-branch-name>`.) [nutshell.003-switching-branches]\n\n- If you prefer to create the branch first and switch later, run  \n\n  ```bash\n  git branch <new-branch-name>\n  git checkout <new-branch-name>\n  ```  \n\n  or, using the newer command,  \n\n  ```bash\n  git branch <new-branch-name>\n  git switch <new-branch-name>\n  ```  \n\n  The first command creates the branch; the second switches the working directory to it. [nutshell.003-switching-branches]\n\n[NOTE] (MEDIUM RISK): Command(s) `git checkout` can rewrite history -- use with caution.",
